  10. HONEST LIMITS OF THIS EVIDENCE
  This controller is tested AGAINST MOCKS, not against a running engine. The fixture builds a standalone
  mock web request harness around the controller and injects a mocked task service, so what the two test
  methods below observe is the controller's own behaviour on a stubbed collaborator. No engine is started,
  no process is deployed and no database is touched. That is the correct shape for a unit test, and it is
  also the precise reason this report cannot be read as engine coverage.
  Five integration classes in this module produce NO file in this folder: CandidateGroupIT,
  TaskActivitiIT, TaskDiagramIT, BuckslipActivitiIT and BuckslipArkcaseIT, re-derived with
  find acm-plugins/acm-default-plugins/acm-task-plugin/src/test/java -name '*IT.java'.
  A unit test run stops at the test phase, while the integration test runner's goals bind later: the root
  aggregator declares that runner with no explicit phase, so both of its goals take their default phases,
  and both of those fall after the test phase. An absent integration test is neither a pass nor a failure
  and may NEVER justify a test exclusion.
  Those five classes are precisely the ones that exercise the workflow engine end to end. The module's most
  engine proximate unit test, the workflow engine data access test, is additionally dormant at the
  baseline under a pre-existing class level ignore annotation, and a dormant test is not a baseline
  failure. Therefore the unit test evidence in this folder does NOT clear the workflow engine residual
  risk, and this report does not clear it either, notwithstanding that this class sits on that engine's
  API level surface and is the member that starts engine work. That risk is assigned to the deployment and
  smoke gates; this folder supplies the module level before picture only, and overstating it would amount
  to a false clearance. The workflow engine is the oldest load bearing engine in the reactor, drives 36
  live process definitions, and could not be bootstrapped for testing without a database, so it is
  classified as residual unverified risk rather than declared safe. Its version does not change: a bump
  without a demonstrated incompatibility is out of scope just as firmly as an unjustified omission. Three
  findings bound that concern without eliminating it: the process definition corpus declares zero script
  tasks and zero script formats, so the engine never asks for a scripting engine and the platform's
  removal of the bundled one cannot reach it; the engine's persistence layer reflects over the
  application's own domain classes through ordinary unrestricted reflection; and it reads no class bytes
  and touches no platform internal packages.
  Because that engine version is constant, and because this module's source and POM are untouched, any
  difference between this report and its migrated counterpart points at the runtime itself or at a root
  level library change: the mocking library discussed above, the Spring test runner this class uses, or
  the expression language library on the decision table path, whose accessor optimizer failure reproduced
  even against a bean compiled at the JDK 8 target and was therefore a defect in that library's own
  generated code rather than in the classes it reads.

  11. TWO FALSE POSITIVES A REVIEWER SHOULD NOT DRAW
  First, this reactor contains NO test retry tooling. A recursive search of all 145 POMs for a rerun count
  setting returns zero, and no retry plugin is declared anywhere, so a failure observed at the baseline is
  real and reproducible, and failing fast is safe. The reactor does carry a Spring retry APPLICATION
  library, declared as a root version property and consumed by exactly three child modules, which has
  nothing whatever to do with rerunning tests. In a task and workflow module, where retry semantics are
  part of the domain vocabulary, that inference is especially tempting and especially wrong.
  Second, coverage exclusions are NOT test exclusions. The root coverage plugin configuration excludes
  eight package globs from coverage MEASUREMENT only, and disables no test. Bindingly related: the coverage
  agent hands its configuration to the test runner through the default argument line property export, so
  the pinned unit test runner declaration must NOT set a literal argument line element. Setting one would
  silently sever instrumentation and leave the coverage check goal evaluating an empty data set, which
  reports a pass while measuring nothing. Instrumentation must be verifiably live. The pinned declaration
  in this reactor sets no such element, and the POM carries an in place comment saying why.

